==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Origin {{unc|en|nocap=1}}. Perhaps from {{der|en|non|skjúta|t=to shoot, dart, move quickly}}, variant of {{m|non|skjóta}}. Compare {{m|non|flytja|t=to move}}. Alternately, perhaps a {{glossary|back-formation}} from {{m|en|skittish}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=skit|pos=''v.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=181041|date=March 2023}}</ref> which in turn may derive from Old Norse or another {{w|North Germanic language}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|skittish|adj|181052|March 2023}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-us-skit.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪt|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[short]] [[comic]] [[performance]].
# A [[jeer]] or [[sally]]; a brief [[satire]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1882|author={{w|Leslie Stephen}}|title=Swift
|passage=That is a mere '''skit''' compared with this strange performance.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[wanton]] [[girl]]; a [[wench]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1936|title=Like the Phoenix|author=Anthony Bertram
|passage=However, terrible as it may seem to the tall maiden sisters of J.P.'s in Queen Anne houses with walled vegetable gardens, this courtesan, strumpet, harlot, whore, punk, fille de joie, street-walker, this trollop, this trull, this baggage, this hussy, this drab, '''skit''', rig, quean, mopsy, demirep, demimondaine, this wanton, this fornicatress, this doxy, this concubine, this frail sister, this poor Queenie&mdash;did actually solicit me, did actually say 'coming home to-night, dearie' and my soul was not blasted enough to call a policeman.}}

